<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Example of graph satisfying <math id="S2.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}(G)=\left\lceil\frac{{\rm conf}(G)}{2\Delta-1}\right\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>conf</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}(G)=\left\lceil\frac{{\rm conf}(G)}{2\Delta-1}\right\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The bound in Remark <a href="#Thmremark1" title="Remark 1 ‣ 2 Preliminaries, first results and a conjecture ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is very general, and it can be bad for some graphs.
As an illustration, consider <math id="S2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n,n}</annotation></semantics></math>: the bound we obtain is <math id="S2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}(K_{n,n})\geq\frac{n^{2}}{2n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}(K_{n,n})\geq\frac{n^{2}}{2n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Later on, we will show this is distant from the optimal by about a factor of <math id="S2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.
There are other contexts, however, in which this bound is not too bad, as we will see later on with the case of paths and cycles.
Nevertheless, the bound in Remark <a href="#Thmremark1" title="Remark 1 ‣ 2 Preliminaries, first results and a conjecture ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> can be tight for some graphs, as attested by the graph shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Preliminaries, first results and a conjecture ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>FPT algorithms</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we provide our efficient algorithms for computing an optimal edge-irregulator of a given graph.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Kernelization by solution size plus the maximum degree</h3>

<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Irregularity-Deletion<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has a kernel of size <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(k\Delta^{2k+2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(k\Delta^{2k+2})</annotation></semantics></math> parameterized by the solution size <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> plus the maximum degree <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> of the input graph <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus an optimal edge-irregulator of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{FPT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖥𝖯𝖳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{FPT}</annotation></semantics></math>-time.</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproofx2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmproofx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,k)</annotation></semantics></math> be an instance of <span id="Thmproofx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Irregularity-Deletion</span>. Let <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> be the maximum degree of <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. For an edge <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in E(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in E(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote by <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(e,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(e,r)</annotation></semantics></math> the ball of centre <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> and radius <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, which is defined as the set of vertices at distance at most <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> from an endpoint of <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We first define the set <math id="Thmproofx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{C}=\{uv\in E(G):d_{G}(u)=d_{G}(v)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{C}=\{uv\in E(G):d_{G}(u)=d_{G}(v)\}</annotation></semantics></math>. That is, <math id="Thmproofx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the edges in conflict of <math id="Thmproofx2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that, due to Observation <a href="#Thmremark1" title="Remark 1 ‣ 2 Preliminaries, first results and a conjecture ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, if <math id="Thmproofx2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|E_{C}|={\rm conf}(G)\geq k(2\Delta+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>conf</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|E_{C}|={\rm conf}(G)\geq k(2\Delta+1)</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="Thmproofx2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,k)</annotation></semantics></math> is a no-instance of <span id="Thmproofx2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Irregularity-Deletion</span>. Thus, if <math id="Thmproofx2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|E_{C}|\geq k(2\Delta+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|E_{C}|\geq k(2\Delta+1)</annotation></semantics></math>, returns <math id="Thmproofx2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{2},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{2},0)</annotation></semantics></math> as a kernel.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Otherwise for each <math id="Thmproofx2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in E_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in E_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define by <math id="Thmproofx2.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V_{e}=\{v\in B(e,2k+1)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{e}=\{v\in B(e,2k+1)\}</annotation></semantics></math> the set of vertices at distance at most <math id="Thmproofx2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k+1</annotation></semantics></math> from an endpoint of <math id="Thmproofx2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>. We finally define <math id="Thmproofx2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{H}=\underset{e\in E_{C}}{\cup}V_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mo lspace="0em">∪</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">e</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">E</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">C</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{H}=\underset{e\in E_{C}}{\cup}V_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> and by <math id="Thmproofx2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=G[V_{H}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=G[V_{H}]</annotation></semantics></math> the subgraph of <math id="Thmproofx2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> induced by <math id="Thmproofx2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, for each vertex <math id="Thmproofx2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmproofx2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="Thmproofx2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{H}(u)\neq d_{G}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{H}(u)\neq d_{G}(u)</annotation></semantics></math>, we add leaves in <math id="Thmproofx2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and connect them to <math id="Thmproofx2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> so that it has the same degree in <math id="Thmproofx2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and in <math id="Thmproofx2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote by <math id="Thmproofx2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> the set of leaves added during this step. Note that this step cannot add conflicts since all the new vertices added have degree one, and since all the vertices of <math id="Thmproofx2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> were already in the connected component of a conflict, they had degree at least one before adding leaves connected to them. Moreover, note by definition, if a vertex is at distance at most <math id="Thmproofx2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k-1</annotation></semantics></math> from a conflict <math id="Thmproofx2.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>, then all its neighbours are in <math id="Thmproofx2.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the only vertices on which leaves are added are at distance at least <math id="Thmproofx2.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k</annotation></semantics></math> from a any conflict. The resulting graph <math id="Thmproofx2.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is a kernel. It remains to prove that <math id="Thmproofx2.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H,k)</annotation></semantics></math> is a yes instance of <span id="Thmproofx2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Irregularity-Deletion</span> if and only if <math id="Thmproofx2.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is and to compute the size of <math id="Thmproofx2.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_numclaim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim 3.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has at most <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k(2\Delta+1)\Delta^{2k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k(2\Delta+1)\Delta^{2k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> vertices</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmproofx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproofx2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof of the claim.</span>
Each <math id="Thmproofx2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> is constructed starting from two vertices, and adding at most <math id="Thmproofx2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> new vertices attached to each of them each time the diameter increase. Thus, each <math id="Thmproofx2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> contains at most <math id="Thmproofx2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\Delta^{2k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\Delta^{2k}</annotation></semantics></math> vertices. Thus, the union of the <math id="Thmproofx2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> contains at most <math id="Thmproofx2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k(2\Delta+1)\Delta^{2k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k(2\Delta+1)\Delta^{2k}</annotation></semantics></math> vertices. Finally, we add at most <math id="Thmproofx2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> leaves connected to each of them, which ensure that <math id="Thmproofx2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V(H)|\leq 2k(2\Delta+1)\Delta^{2k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V(H)|\leq 2k(2\Delta+1)\Delta^{2k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
 <math id="Thmproofx2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diamond" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋄</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diamond</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_numclaim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim 3.3</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,k)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a yes instance <span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Irregularity-Deletion</span> if and only if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H,k)</annotation></semantics></math> is.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmproofx2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproofx2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof of the claim.</span>
Suppose first that <math id="Thmproofx2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H,k)</annotation></semantics></math> is a yes-instance of <span id="Thmproofx2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Irregularity-Deletion</span>. Let <math id="Thmproofx2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be an optimal edge-irregulator of <math id="Thmproofx2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. Since all the vertices of <math id="Thmproofx2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> are at distance at least <math id="Thmproofx2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k</annotation></semantics></math> from any conflict, using Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1 ‣ 3.1 Kernelization by solution size plus the maximum degree ‣ 3 FPT algorithms ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we know that no edge of <math id="Thmproofx2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent to <math id="Thmproofx2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, all the edges of <math id="Thmproofx2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> are between vertices that are also in <math id="Thmproofx2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, in <math id="Thmproofx2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-S</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="Thmproofx2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge, if both <math id="Thmproofx2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> are at distance at most <math id="Thmproofx2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k-1</annotation></semantics></math> from a conflict, they have the same degree in <math id="Thmproofx2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-S</annotation></semantics></math> and in <math id="Thmproofx2.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H-S</annotation></semantics></math>, thus they are not in conflict, and if they are both at distance at least <math id="Thmproofx2.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k</annotation></semantics></math> from a conflict, no edge of <math id="Thmproofx2.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent neither to <math id="Thmproofx2.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> nor to <math id="Thmproofx2.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, thus their degree has not changed, and since they were not in conflict in <math id="Thmproofx2.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, they are not in conflict in <math id="Thmproofx2.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose now that <math id="Thmproofx2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,k)</annotation></semantics></math> is a yes-instance of <span id="Thmproofx2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Irregularity-Deletion</span>. Let <math id="Thmproofx2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be an optimal edge-irregulator of <math id="Thmproofx2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1 ‣ 3.1 Kernelization by solution size plus the maximum degree ‣ 3 FPT algorithms ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, all the edges of <math id="Thmproofx2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> are at distance at most <math id="Thmproofx2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k-1</annotation></semantics></math> from a conflict, Thus, they are all in <math id="Thmproofx2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. In <math id="Thmproofx2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H-S</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="Thmproofx2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge, if <math id="Thmproofx2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmproofx2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="Thmproofx2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, the other endpoint of <math id="Thmproofx2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv</annotation></semantics></math> is at distance exactly <math id="Thmproofx2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k+1</annotation></semantics></math> from the nearest conflict. Thus, all its neighbours are at distance at least <math id="Thmproofx2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k</annotation></semantics></math> from the nearest conflict, and thus his degree has not changed, thus <math id="Thmproofx2.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv</annotation></semantics></math> is not a conflict in <math id="Thmproofx2.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H-S</annotation></semantics></math>. Otherwise, we have <math id="Thmproofx2.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G-S}(u)=d_{H-S}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G-S}(u)=d_{H-S}(u)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx2.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G-S}(v)=d_{H-S}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G-S}(v)=d_{H-S}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, since <math id="Thmproofx2.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-S</annotation></semantics></math> is locally irregular, <math id="Thmproofx2.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be a conflict.
 <math id="Thmproofx2.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diamond" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋄</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diamond</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, <math id="Thmproofx2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,K)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H,k)</annotation></semantics></math> are equivalent instances of <span id="Thmproofx2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Irregularity-Deletion</span>, thus <math id="Thmproofx2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is a kernel and has size <math id="Thmproofx2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(k\Delta^{2k+2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(k\Delta^{2k+2})</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The kernel that will be used in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 ‣ 3.1 Kernelization by solution size plus the maximum degree ‣ 3 FPT algorithms ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> will be mostly due to the following lemma, and the fact that we can bound the number of vertices in a graph in function of its diameter and its maximum degree. Thus, we prove here the lemma and postpone the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 ‣ 3.1 Kernelization by solution size plus the maximum degree ‣ 3 FPT algorithms ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> to the appendix.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph, and let <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be an optimal edge-irregulator of <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. For any edge <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in S</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists an edge <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv\in E</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(u)=d(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(u)=d(v)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is at distance at most <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2|S|-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2|S|-1</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproofx3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmproofx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proof is by induction on <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=|S|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=|S|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Assume first that <math id="Thmproofx3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="Thmproofx3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\{e\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\{e\}</annotation></semantics></math> is an optimal edge-irregulator and is not empty, there exists at least one conflict <math id="Thmproofx3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv\in E</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, since <math id="Thmproofx3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-\{e\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-\{e\}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally irregular, either <math id="Thmproofx3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=uv</annotation></semantics></math> or one vertex among <math id="Thmproofx3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmproofx3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> has a different degree in <math id="Thmproofx3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-S</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies that <math id="Thmproofx3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is either adjacent to <math id="Thmproofx3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> or to <math id="Thmproofx3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus at distance at most <math id="Thmproofx3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="Thmproofx3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose now <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.Let <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv</annotation></semantics></math> be a conflict in <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Since in <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-S</annotation></semantics></math>, the vertices <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> are not in conflict, there exists one edge <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> that is adjacent to <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> or to <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider now the graph <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-\{e\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-\{e\}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the optimality of <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, we get that <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S-\{e\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S-\{e\}</annotation></semantics></math> is an optimal edge-irregulator of <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-\{e\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-\{e\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus by induction hypothesis, all the edges of <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> are at distance at most <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2|S-\{e\}|-1=2|S|-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2|S-\{e\}|-1=2|S|-3</annotation></semantics></math> from a conflict in <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-\{e\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-\{e\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider now a conflict <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ab" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ab</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-\{e\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-\{e\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then either <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ab" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ab</annotation></semantics></math> was already a conflict in <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ab" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ab</annotation></semantics></math> has become a conflict due to the deletion of <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>. This means that either <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent to wither <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> or to <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ab" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ab</annotation></semantics></math> is at distance at most <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, using the triangular inequality, any edge of <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is at distance at most <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k-3+2=2k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k-3+2=2k-1</annotation></semantics></math> from a conflict of <math id="Thmproofx3.p3.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Vertex cover number</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While it is already known that <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{e}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{e}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed in <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{FPT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖥𝖯𝖳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{FPT}</annotation></semantics></math>-time parameterized by the vertex cover number using the <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{FPT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖥𝖯𝖳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{FPT}</annotation></semantics></math>-algorithm for the more general parameter of <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">vertex integrity</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, we present here an algorithm that is tailor-made for the vertex cover number, and, as such, is faster.</p>
</div>
