<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Structural results</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we investigate common classes of graphs to determine their optimal edge-irregulators. Our investigation is lead by Conjecture <a href="#Thmconjecture1" title="Conjecture 1 ‣ 2 Preliminaries, first results and a conjecture ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, which we verify for most of the considered classes. The results in this section are important in order to gain a better understanding of <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> in regards to structural parameters, but also since general bounds on <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> can be deduced from simpler cases:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph and <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be a set of edges of <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-S</annotation></semantics></math> consists of <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> connected components <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1},\dots,G_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1},\dots,G_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}(G)\leq|S|+\sum_{i=1}^{p}{\rm I}_{e}(G_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}(G)\leq|S|+\sum_{i=1}^{p}{\rm I}_{e}(G_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We start by considering graphs with maximum degree <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> (paths and cycles).
For <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote by <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> the path of order <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, and, for <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, by <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> the cycle of order <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this case, we are able to determine <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> completely. Roughly, to prove this result we delete the minimum number of edges to reach as many disjoint <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>s as possible.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</span></p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}(P_{n})=\frac{n-1}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}(P_{n})=\frac{n-1}{3}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> if </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\equiv 1\bmod 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>mod</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\equiv 1\bmod 3</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}(P_{n})=\lceil\frac{n-1}{3}\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}(P_{n})=\lceil\frac{n-1}{3}\rceil</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> if </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\equiv 2\bmod 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>mod</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\equiv 2\bmod 3</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}(P_{n})=\lfloor\frac{n-1}{3}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}(P_{n})=\lfloor\frac{n-1}{3}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> otherwise.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, for every <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}(C_{n})={\rm I}_{e}(P_{n})+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}(C_{n})={\rm I}_{e}(P_{n})+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproofx5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmproofx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the only value of <math id="Thmproofx5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmproofx5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally irregular is <math id="Thmproofx5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=3</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, for any set <math id="Thmproofx5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subseteq E(P_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subseteq E(P_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="Thmproofx5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}-S</annotation></semantics></math> to be locally irregular, every non-trivial connected component must contain exactly two edges. In particular, <math id="Thmproofx5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally irregular, so <math id="Thmproofx5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}(P_{3})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}(P_{3})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let us denote by <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1},e_{2},\dots,e_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1},e_{2},\dots,e_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> the consecutive edges of <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, from one end-vertex to the other. Set <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}=\{e_{1},e_{2},e_{3}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}=\{e_{1},e_{2},e_{3}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{2}=\{e_{4},e_{5},e_{6}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{2}=\{e_{4},e_{5},e_{6}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and so on; that is, let <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1},\dots,E_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1},\dots,E_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> be consecutive groups of three consecutive edges of <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the last group, <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, might contain one (if <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1\equiv 1\bmod 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>mod</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1\equiv 1\bmod 3</annotation></semantics></math>) or two (if <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1\equiv 2\bmod 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>mod</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1\equiv 2\bmod 3</annotation></semantics></math>) edges only. Note that <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=\lceil\frac{n-1}{3}\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=\lceil\frac{n-1}{3}\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>. For lower bounds, observe that, for any set <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subseteq E(P_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subseteq E(P_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}-S</annotation></semantics></math> is locally irregular, at least one edge of each of <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1},\dots,E_{p-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1},\dots,E_{p-1}</annotation></semantics></math> must belong to <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. This is because, otherwise, <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}-S</annotation></semantics></math> would have a connected component containing at least three edges, a contradiction as mentioned earlier. Now, if <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1\equiv 0\bmod 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>mod</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1\equiv 0\bmod 3</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=\frac{n-1}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=\frac{n-1}{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S|\geq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S|\geq p</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1\equiv 2\bmod 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>mod</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1\equiv 2\bmod 3</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S|\geq\lfloor\frac{n-1}{3}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S|\geq\lfloor\frac{n-1}{3}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>. It remains to consider when <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1\equiv 1\bmod 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>mod</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1\equiv 1\bmod 3</annotation></semantics></math>, in which case we similarly obtain <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S|\geq\lfloor\frac{n-1}{3}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S|\geq\lfloor\frac{n-1}{3}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> only. We claim <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> must contain at least one additional edge. If <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> (the only edge of <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>), then we have our conclusion. Otherwise, by earlier arguments we deduce that <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> must contain <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{n-3},e_{n-6},\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{n-3},e_{n-6},\dots</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="Thmproofx5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, the second edge of each of <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1},\dots,E_{p-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1},\dots,E_{p-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> does not contain any other edge, then we deduce that <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is part of <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> while <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is not, which contradicts that <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}-S</annotation></semantics></math> is locally irregular. Thus, we must have <math id="Thmproofx5.p2.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S|\geq\lceil\frac{n-1}{3}\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S|\geq\lceil\frac{n-1}{3}\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Regarding upper bounds, let us consider <math id="Thmproofx5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, the set of edges of <math id="Thmproofx5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> comprising every three edges of <math id="Thmproofx5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, namely <math id="Thmproofx5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{3},e_{6},e_{9},\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>9</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{3},e_{6},e_{9},\dots</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="Thmproofx5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1\equiv 0\bmod 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>mod</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1\equiv 0\bmod 3</annotation></semantics></math>, then note that <math id="Thmproofx5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="Thmproofx5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="Thmproofx5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1\equiv 2\bmod 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>mod</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1\equiv 2\bmod 3</annotation></semantics></math>, then the last edge of <math id="Thmproofx5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmproofx5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmproofx5.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{n-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{n-3}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="Thmproofx5.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1\equiv 1\bmod 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>mod</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1\equiv 1\bmod 3</annotation></semantics></math>, the last edge in <math id="Thmproofx5.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmproofx5.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{n-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{n-2}</annotation></semantics></math>; in that special case, we also add <math id="Thmproofx5.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Thmproofx5.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. In all three cases, it can be checked that <math id="Thmproofx5.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}-S</annotation></semantics></math> is locally irregular, essentially because for every three consecutive edges of <math id="Thmproofx5.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> there is one in <math id="Thmproofx5.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Now:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">if <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1\equiv 0\bmod 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>mod</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1\equiv 0\bmod 3</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S|=\frac{n-1}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S|=\frac{n-1}{3}</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">if <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1\equiv 1\bmod 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>mod</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1\equiv 1\bmod 3</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S|=\lfloor\frac{n-1}{3}\rfloor+1=\lceil\frac{n-1}{3}\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S|=\lfloor\frac{n-1}{3}\rfloor+1=\lceil\frac{n-1}{3}\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">if <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1\equiv 2\bmod 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>mod</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1\equiv 2\bmod 3</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S|=\lfloor\frac{n-1}{3}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S|=\lfloor\frac{n-1}{3}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx5.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmproofx5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, we have the claimed upper bounds, and all equalities hold.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="Thmproofx5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a cycle, all its vertices have degree <math id="Thmproofx5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, thus, at least one edge has to be removed from <math id="Thmproofx5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. After this first edge removed, we are left to a path on <math id="Thmproofx5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices, which concludes the proof.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we deal with complete bipartite graphs. Recall that <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n,m}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the complete bipartite graph with parts of size <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n,m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n,m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n,m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n,m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</span></p>
<ul id="S4.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}(K_{n,m})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}(K_{n,m})=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> if </span><math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\neq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\neq m</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}(K_{n,m})=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}(K_{n,m})=n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> otherwise.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproofx6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmproofx6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The first item is because <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n,m}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally irregular whenever <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\neq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\neq m</annotation></semantics></math>. We now focus on <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n,n}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Choose any vertex, <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, of <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be the set containing all edges incident to <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, clearly, <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n,n}-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n,n}-S</annotation></semantics></math> is locally irregular, and <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}(K_{n,n})\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}(K_{n,n})\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>. To see, now, that <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}(K_{n,n})\geq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}(K_{n,n})\geq n</annotation></semantics></math>, consider, towards a contradiction, any set <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of at most <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> edges such that <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n,n}-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n,n}-S</annotation></semantics></math> is locally irregular. Denoting by <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U,V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U,V)</annotation></semantics></math> the bipartition of <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n,n}</annotation></semantics></math> (where, thus, <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|U|=|V|=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|U|=|V|=n</annotation></semantics></math>), since <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S|\leq n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S|\leq n-1</annotation></semantics></math>, there must be at least a <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in U</annotation></semantics></math> not incident to any edge of <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, and similarly a <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> not incident to any edge of <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{K_{n,n}-S}(u)=d_{K_{n,n}-S}(v)=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{K_{n,n}-S}(u)=d_{K_{n,n}-S}(v)=n</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> are adjacent in <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n,n}-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n,n}-S</annotation></semantics></math>; this is a contradiction.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We now move towards more general families of graphs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.3</span></span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> is a connected bipartite graph of order <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and minimum degree <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}(G)\leq n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}(G)\leq n-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproofx7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmproofx7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> be any degree-<math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> vertex of <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1},V_{2},\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1},V_{2},\dots</annotation></semantics></math> be the partition of <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)\setminus\{r\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)\setminus\{r\}</annotation></semantics></math> where each <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the vertices of <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> at distance exactly <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. Our goal is to construct a set <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of edges of <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> such that, in <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-S</annotation></semantics></math>, the vertices in some <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> even have even degrees, while those in some <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> odd have odd degrees (except maybe for vertices becoming isolated, and one vertex; see later). Towards this, we start with <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> being empty. Assuming <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)\setminus\{r\}=V_{1}\cup\dots\cup V_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)\setminus\{r\}=V_{1}\cup\dots\cup V_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="Thmproofx7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, the maximum distance between <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and a vertex of <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>), we consider the vertices of <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)\setminus\{r\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)\setminus\{r\}</annotation></semantics></math> one by one, following the parts <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{d},V_{d-1},\dots,V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d},V_{d-1},\dots,V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in this order. For any vertex <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\neq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\neq r</annotation></semantics></math> considered this way, we consider <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G-S}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G-S}(u)</annotation></semantics></math>, and, in case this degree does not have the required parity, we add to <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> a single edge <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uu^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uu^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is closer to <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> than <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is (<math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> exists since <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\neq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\neq r</annotation></semantics></math>). As a result, after having treated <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, note that <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G-S}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G-S}(u)</annotation></semantics></math> (for the updated <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>) is as desired modulo <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Once all vertices of <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{d},\dots,V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d},\dots,V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> have been treated this way, note that, for the resulting <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G-S}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G-S}(u)</annotation></semantics></math> has the desired parity for every <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in V(G)\setminus\{r\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in V(G)\setminus\{r\}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, for any two adjacent vertices <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\not\in\{u,u^{\prime}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\not\in\{u,u^{\prime}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G-S}(u)\not\equiv d_{G-S}(u^{\prime})\bmod 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≢</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G-S}(u)\not\equiv d_{G-S}(u^{\prime})\bmod 2</annotation></semantics></math>. So the only reason why <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-S</annotation></semantics></math> might not be locally irregular, is because <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> has the same (non-zero) degree as its unique neighbour, <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, this means <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="rw\not\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">rw\not\in S</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is the only neighbour of <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-S</annotation></semantics></math>. In that case, we simply add <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="rw" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">rw</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> to get rid of this conflict, without creating new ones; then, for the resulting <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-S</annotation></semantics></math> is locally irregular. In all cases, we note that the eventual <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S|\leq n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S|\leq n-1</annotation></semantics></math>: indeed, <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> was constructed by considering every vertex (different from <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>) exactly once, and adding to <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> at most one incident edge on a shortest path to <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="Thmproofx7.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}(G)\leq n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}(G)\leq n-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Adapting the above strategy, we can prove that:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.4</span></span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every connected graph <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> of order <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> edges and maximum degree <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}(G)\leq\left\lfloor\frac{m}{2}\right\rfloor+n+\Delta-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}(G)\leq\left\lfloor\frac{m}{2}\right\rfloor+n+\Delta-2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproofx8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmproofx8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=(U,V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=(U,V)</annotation></semantics></math> be a maximum cut of <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="Thmproofx8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, a partition of <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> maximising the number <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> of edges from <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>. It is well known that <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\geq\lceil m/2\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\geq\lceil m/2\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>, essentially because, for every vertex <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in U</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math>), we have <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{V}(u)\geq d_{U}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{V}(u)\geq d_{U}(u)</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{U}(v)\geq d_{V}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{U}(v)\geq d_{V}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>, resp.) – indeed, otherwise we would contradict the maximality of <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> when swapping <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, resp.). We set <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(G)=B\cup R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(G)=B\cup R</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> contains all edges joining <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> contains all other edges (each included in either <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Set <math id="Thmproofx8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=G-R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=G-R</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, <math id="Thmproofx8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is bipartite. Note that <math id="Thmproofx8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is not necessarily connected. However, since <math id="Thmproofx8.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is connected, there must be two (bipartite) connected components <math id="Thmproofx8.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx8.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmproofx8.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> that are joined by an edge <math id="Thmproofx8.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in R</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmproofx8.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>; then, we just move <math id="Thmproofx8.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="Thmproofx8.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Thmproofx8.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. Since adding an edge joining two disjoint bipartite graphs results in a bipartite graph, we still have that <math id="Thmproofx8.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-R</annotation></semantics></math> is bipartite. So, by repeating this process, we end up with a bipartition <math id="Thmproofx8.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\cup R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\cup R</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmproofx8.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(G)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmproofx8.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=G-R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=G-R</annotation></semantics></math> is a connected bipartite graph, and we still have <math id="Thmproofx8.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|R|\leq\lfloor m/2\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|R|\leq\lfloor m/2\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> (since we perhaps moved edges from <math id="Thmproofx8.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Thmproofx8.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> in the process).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let now <math id="Thmproofx8.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> be any vertex of <math id="Thmproofx8.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> that is not a cut-vertex of <math id="Thmproofx8.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> (for instance, any last vertex encountered during the run of a BFS algorithm is a candidate as <math id="Thmproofx8.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>). Since <math id="Thmproofx8.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is not a cut-vertex of <math id="Thmproofx8.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, by removing any set <math id="Thmproofx8.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmproofx8.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(r)-1\leq\Delta-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(r)-1\leq\Delta-1</annotation></semantics></math> edges incident to <math id="Thmproofx8.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain a bipartite graph <math id="Thmproofx8.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmproofx8.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(H^{\prime})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(H^{\prime})=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 4.3 ‣ 4 Structural results ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> tells us that <math id="Thmproofx8.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}(H^{\prime})\leq n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}(H^{\prime})\leq n-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx8.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx8.p4.1" class="ltx_p">If we denote by <math id="Thmproofx8.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> any optimal edge-irregulator of <math id="Thmproofx8.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, then note that <math id="Thmproofx8.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-R-X-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-R-X-S</annotation></semantics></math> is locally irregular. As a result,</p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\rm I}_{e}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\rm I}_{e}(G)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq|R\cup X\cup S|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq|R\cup X\cup S|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\left\lfloor\frac{m}{2}\right\rfloor+(\Delta-1)+(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\left\lfloor\frac{m}{2}\right\rfloor+(\Delta-1)+(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left\lfloor\frac{m}{2}\right\rfloor+n+\Delta-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left\lfloor\frac{m}{2}\right\rfloor+n+\Delta-2</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx8.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmproofx8.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This concludes the proof.</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Trees</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now focus on trees. We first prove that Conjecture <a href="#Thmconjecture1" title="Conjecture 1 ‣ 2 Preliminaries, first results and a conjecture ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> holds for this family, and then provide a linear-time algorithm for trees of bounded maximum degree.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We proceed in two steps: we first deal with “star-like” trees, before treating the more general case. Recall that a <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">bistar</span> is a tree with exactly two vertices <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> of degree at least <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> that are adjacent. See Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.1 Trees ‣ 4 Structural results ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> for a representation of a bistar, as well as some intuition on the proof of the following lemma. For the more general case, we also need to observe a lemma concerning bridges.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a subdivision of a star or of a bistar, then <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}(G)\leq\frac{1}{3}|E(G)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}(G)\leq\frac{1}{3}|E(G)|</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproofx9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmproofx9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that the distance from an edge <math id="Thmproofx9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> to a vertex <math id="Thmproofx9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is the minimum distance between the endpoints of <math id="Thmproofx9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. First assume that <math id="Thmproofx9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a star with a center <math id="Thmproofx9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> of degree at least <math id="Thmproofx9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>. Without loss of generality, we can assume that branches are of length at most <math id="Thmproofx9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> (otherwise we remove the edge at distance 2 from the end point of such a branch to disconnect a <math id="Thmproofx9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> an proceed on the component containing <math id="Thmproofx9.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>). We consider <math id="Thmproofx9.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> to be the set of all edges at distance <math id="Thmproofx9.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="Thmproofx9.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, which is obviously an edge-irregulator of <math id="Thmproofx9.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> (since all degree-two vertices of <math id="Thmproofx9.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-S</annotation></semantics></math> are adjacent to <math id="Thmproofx9.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and a vertex of degree <math id="Thmproofx9.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>). Note that <math id="Thmproofx9.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S|</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the number of branches with exactly three edges, and all those branches are edge-disjoint, hence the result. We can now assume <math id="Thmproofx9.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a subdivided bistar other than a star, with centers <math id="Thmproofx9.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v</annotation></semantics></math>, both of degree at least <math id="Thmproofx9.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Assume first that <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v</annotation></semantics></math> are not adjacent in <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> (hence, the edge between <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v</annotation></semantics></math> in the original bistar has been divided). We denote <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math> the path between <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{\prime},v^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{\prime},v^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> the vertices of <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math> adjacent to <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v</annotation></semantics></math> respectively (where <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> can be the same vertex if <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(u,v)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(u,v)=2</annotation></semantics></math>). We find a set of edge <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math> such that:</p>
<ul id="S4.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">for any two adjacent vertices <math id="S4.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w,w^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w,w^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math> other than <math id="S4.I4.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{\prime},v^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{\prime},v^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, we guarantee <math id="S4.I4.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I4.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> have distinct degree in <math id="S4.I4.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{uv}-S{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{uv}-S{uv}</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uu^{\prime}\notin S_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uu^{\prime}\notin S_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I4.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vv^{\prime}\notin S_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vv^{\prime}\notin S_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I4.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S_{uv}|\leq\frac{1}{3}|E(P_{uv})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S_{uv}|\leq\frac{1}{3}|E(P_{uv})|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="Thmproofx9.p2.2" class="ltx_p">If <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|E(P_{uv})|=0\mod 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>mod</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|E(P_{uv})|=0\mod 3</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\mod 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>mod</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\mod 3</annotation></semantics></math>, then we consider <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{u},e_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{u},e_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> the two edges at distance <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v</annotation></semantics></math> respectively in <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{uv}-\{e_{u},e_{v}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{uv}-\{e_{u},e_{v}\}</annotation></semantics></math> has at most three connected components, the one containing <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uu^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uu^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vv^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vv^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>) denoted <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{u^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{u^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{v^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{v^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>) as well as <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> a (possibly empty) path of length <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\mod 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>mod</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\mod 3</annotation></semantics></math>. We then apply the result of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 ‣ 4 Structural results ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> to obtain <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> an edge-irregulator of <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> of size at most <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{3}|E(P)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{3}|E(P)|</annotation></semantics></math> and denote <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math> the set <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{p}\cup\{e_{u},e_{v}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{p}\cup\{e_{u},e_{v}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|E(P_{uv})|=1\mod 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>mod</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|E(P_{uv})|=1\mod 3</annotation></semantics></math>, then we instead consider <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the edge at distance <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> instead, and the same process applies. It is easy to check that in both cases, all the above conditions are fulfilled.
Finally, we consider <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{u},G_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{u},G_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> each component of <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-P_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-P_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math> and apply the exact same process as before on <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{u}\cup P_{u^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{u}\cup P_{u^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{v}\cup P_{v^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{v}\cup P_{v^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> (which are both subdivided stars), yielding two optimal edge-irregulators <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{u},S_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{u},S_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that this process does not alter the degree of neither of <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> nor <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{u}\cup S_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{u}\cup S_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge-irregulator of <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S_{u}\cup S_{v}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S_{u}\cup S_{v}|</annotation></semantics></math> it still the number of branches of length exactly <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. We can now assume that <math id="Thmproofx9.p2.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v</annotation></semantics></math> are adjacent.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx9.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx9.p3.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="Thmproofx9.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(u)\neq d(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(u)\neq d(v)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx9.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(u),d(v)\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(u),d(v)\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, then we consider <math id="Thmproofx9.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{u},G_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{u},G_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> each component of <math id="Thmproofx9.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-uv</annotation></semantics></math> and apply the exact same process as before on <math id="Thmproofx9.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{u}\cup uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{u}\cup uv</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx9.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{v}\cup uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{v}\cup uv</annotation></semantics></math> (which are both subdivided stars), yielding two optimal edge-irregulators <math id="Thmproofx9.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{u},S_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{u},S_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that this process does not alter the degree of neither of <math id="Thmproofx9.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> nor <math id="Thmproofx9.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="Thmproofx9.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{u}\cup S_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{u}\cup S_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge-irregulator of <math id="Thmproofx9.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, <math id="Thmproofx9.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S_{u}\cup S_{v}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S_{u}\cup S_{v}|</annotation></semantics></math> it still the number of branches of length exactly <math id="Thmproofx9.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmproofx9.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx9.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx9.p4.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="Thmproofx9.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(u)=d(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(u)=d(v)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx9.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(u),d(v)\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(u),d(v)\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, then either there is a branch of length <math id="Thmproofx9.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> (say attached to <math id="Thmproofx9.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>), in which case we disconnect a <math id="Thmproofx9.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> by removing the edge connecting it to <math id="Thmproofx9.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, and fall back into the previous case, or all branches are of length at most <math id="Thmproofx9.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, and we delete an arbitrary branch. Since there are more than <math id="Thmproofx9.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> edges in <math id="Thmproofx9.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, we are done.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx9.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx9.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We can now assume <math id="Thmproofx9.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(u)=d(v)=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(u)=d(v)=3</annotation></semantics></math>. If there is a branch of length <math id="Thmproofx9.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, we disconnect a <math id="Thmproofx9.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and fall back in the case of a star. If there is a branch of length <math id="Thmproofx9.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="Thmproofx9.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uw_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uw_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, then we denote <math id="Thmproofx9.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uw_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uw_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (and <math id="Thmproofx9.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{2}w_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{2}w_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> if such a <math id="Thmproofx9.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="Thmproofx9.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> exists) the edges of the other branch, and consider <math id="Thmproofx9.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\{uw_{2},w_{2}w_{3}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\{uw_{2},w_{2}w_{3}\}</annotation></semantics></math> (if <math id="Thmproofx9.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{2}w_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{2}w_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> exists). Note that if <math id="Thmproofx9.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{2}w_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{2}w_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> exists, there are at least 6 edges in <math id="Thmproofx9.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, thus we are done. We can finally assume that all branches are of length <math id="Thmproofx9.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, and if we denote <math id="Thmproofx9.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1},w_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1},w_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="Thmproofx9.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\prime}_{1},w^{\prime}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\prime}_{1},w^{\prime}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) the vertices on the branches incident to <math id="Thmproofx9.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="Thmproofx9.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\{uw_{1},w_{1}w_{2},w^{\prime}_{1}w^{\prime}_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\{uw_{1},w_{1}w_{2},w^{\prime}_{1}w^{\prime}_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge-irregulator of <math id="Thmproofx9.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="Thmproofx9.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|E(G)|=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|E(G)|=9</annotation></semantics></math>, the proof is complete.</p>
</div>
