<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>A representation of the case where <math id="S4.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(u)=d(v)=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(u)=d(v)=3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v</annotation></semantics></math> are adjacent in the proof of Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 2 ‣ 4.1 Trees ‣ 4 Structural results ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. A white vertex may or may not exist. The dashed edges form an optimal edge-irregulator. In the leftmost figure, we do not provide an edge-irregulator, but rather fall back in the case of a subdivided star.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Thmlemma3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be two edge-irregulators of two graphs <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G,G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G,G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, and let <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be the graph obtained from the disjoint union of <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with the addition of the edge <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I=S\cup S^{\prime}\cup\{uv\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I=S\cup S^{\prime}\cup\{uv\}</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge-irregulator of <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproofx10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmproofx10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider a vertex <math id="Thmproofx10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in V(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in V(H)</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume w.l.o.g. that <math id="Thmproofx10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. It is easy to check that the degree of <math id="Thmproofx10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmproofx10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H-I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H-I</annotation></semantics></math> is the same as in <math id="Thmproofx10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-S</annotation></semantics></math>. The case <math id="Thmproofx10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in V(G^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in V(G^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is similar.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.5</span></span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a tree. Then either <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a path or <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}(T)\leq\frac{1}{3}|E(T)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}(T)\leq\frac{1}{3}|E(T)|</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproofx11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmproofx11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose by contradiction that <math id="Thmproofx11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a minimum counterexample to this theorem. Then <math id="Thmproofx11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be a path, and by Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 2 ‣ 4.1 Trees ‣ 4 Structural results ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <math id="Thmproofx11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be a subdivision of a star or of a bistar. We root <math id="Thmproofx11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> at an arbitrary vertex <math id="Thmproofx11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, and we consider <math id="Thmproofx11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> a deepest vertex of degree <math id="Thmproofx11.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmproofx11.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. If several exist, we choose one of the highest degree. Denote by <math id="Thmproofx11.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> its parent. Note that <math id="Thmproofx11.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\neq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\neq r</annotation></semantics></math>, otherwise the graph would be a subdivided star. If <math id="Thmproofx11.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=r</annotation></semantics></math>, we chose a neighbour of <math id="Thmproofx11.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> other than <math id="Thmproofx11.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> as the new root. For <math id="Thmproofx11.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, we will denote by <math id="Thmproofx11.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> the subtree induced by <math id="Thmproofx11.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> and all its descendant for this rooting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_numclaim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim 4.6</span></span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=(p_{1},p_{2},p_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=(p_{1},p_{2},p_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> be a path of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(p_{1})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(p_{1})=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(p_{2})=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(p_{2})=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(p_{3})\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(p_{3})\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx11.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmproofx11.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproofx11.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof of the claim.</span>
Since <math id="Thmproofx11.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is connected, <math id="Thmproofx11.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(p_{3})\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(p_{3})\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, otherwise we would have <math id="Thmproofx11.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=P</annotation></semantics></math>, which contradicts that <math id="Thmproofx11.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is not a path. Suppose by contradiction <math id="Thmproofx11.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(p_{3})=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(p_{3})=2</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="Thmproofx11.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}=G\setminus T_{p_{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}=G\setminus T_{p_{3}}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="Thmproofx11.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be an edge-irregulator of <math id="Thmproofx11.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. By minimality of <math id="Thmproofx11.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that <math id="Thmproofx11.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S^{\prime}|\leq\frac{|G^{\prime}|}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S^{\prime}|\leq\frac{|G^{\prime}|}{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmproofx11.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{3}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{3}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the parent of <math id="Thmproofx11.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, and consider <math id="Thmproofx11.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=S^{\prime}\cup\{p_{3}p_{3}^{\prime}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=S^{\prime}\cup\{p_{3}p_{3}^{\prime}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="Thmproofx11.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge-irregulator of <math id="Thmproofx11.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> of size at most <math id="Thmproofx11.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{|G|}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{|G|}{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction.
 <math id="Thmproofx11.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diamond" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋄</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diamond</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx11.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx11.p3.1" class="ltx_p">It follows that, by construction, the subtree <math id="Thmproofx11.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> rooted in <math id="Thmproofx11.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is a subdivided star. Let <math id="Thmproofx11.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}=(v_{i,1,},\dots,v_{i,k_{i}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}=(v_{i,1,},\dots,v_{i,k_{i}})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmproofx11.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[k-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[k-1]</annotation></semantics></math> be its branches. Without loss of generality, and according to Claim <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem6" title="Claim 4.6 ‣ Proof ‣ 4.1 Trees ‣ 4 Structural results ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>, we can suppose for <math id="Thmproofx11.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i&lt;j\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i&lt;j\leq k</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmproofx11.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P_{i}|\geq|P_{j}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P_{i}|\geq|P_{j}|</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus <math id="Thmproofx11.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\geq|P_{1}|\geq\dots\geq|P_{k}|\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\geq|P_{1}|\geq\dots\geq|P_{k}|\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<ul id="S4.I5" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:11pt;">
<li id="S4.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S4.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, then by the minimality of <math id="S4.I5.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, we get that <math id="S4.I5.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}=G\setminus T_{u}\cup\{u\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}=G\setminus T_{u}\cup\{u\}</annotation></semantics></math> has an edge-irregulator <math id="S4.I5.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of size at most <math id="S4.I5.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{3}|E(G^{\prime})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{3}|E(G^{\prime})|</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.I5.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv\in S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv\in S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.I5.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\cup S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\cup S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge-irregulator of the required size, where <math id="S4.I5.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge-irregulator of <math id="S4.I5.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> of size at most <math id="S4.I5.i1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{|T_{u}|}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{|T_{u}|}{3}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 2 ‣ 4.1 Trees ‣ 4 Structural results ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Now suppose <math id="S4.I5.i1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv\notin S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∉</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv\notin S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. In <math id="S4.I5.i1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\setminus S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∖</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\setminus S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, the only remaining conflicts can be the edge <math id="S4.I5.i1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv</annotation></semantics></math>. Should this happen, all the edges from <math id="S4.I5.i1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> form an edge-irregulator of <math id="S4.I5.i1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> decreasing the degree of <math id="S4.I5.i1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> by one, thus resolving the conflict <math id="S4.I5.i1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.I5.i1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P_{i}|\leq\frac{|T_{u}|}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P_{i}|\leq\frac{|T_{u}|}{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We can now assume <math id="S4.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math>. There are thus two induced paths <math id="S4.I5.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1},P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1},P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> rooted at <math id="S4.I5.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>. We will distinguish cases depending on the lengths of <math id="S4.I5.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P_{1}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P_{1}|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I5.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P_{2}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P_{2}|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<ul id="S4.I5.i2.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:11pt;">
<li id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P_{1}|=|P_{2}|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P_{1}|=|P_{2}|=1</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}=G\setminus T_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}=G\setminus T_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. By minimality of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, the tree <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a path and has an edge-irregulator <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>of size <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{|G^{\prime}|}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{|G^{\prime}|}{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. By construction, <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=S^{\prime}\cup\{uv\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=S^{\prime}\cup\{uv\}</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge-irregulator of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, we have <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S|=|S^{\prime}|+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S|=|S^{\prime}|+1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|G|=|G^{\prime}|+3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|G|=|G^{\prime}|+3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume now <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P_{1}|=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P_{1}|=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P_{2}|\in\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P_{2}|\in\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the parent of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G}(v)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G}(v)=1</annotation></semantics></math>, then the graph is already locally irregular and we are done. If <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G}(v)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G}(v)=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a path then we are done by Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 2 ‣ 4.1 Trees ‣ 4 Structural results ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Otherwise (if <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G}(v)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G}(v)=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a path),
we consider <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}=G-T_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}=G-T_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be an optimal edge-irregulator of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma3" title="Lemma 3 ‣ 4.1 Trees ‣ 4 Structural results ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the set <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=S^{\prime}\cup\{vv^{\prime}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=S^{\prime}\cup\{vv^{\prime}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge-irregulator of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and has size less than <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{3}|E(G)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{3}|E(G)|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We can now assume <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(v)\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(v)\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. By the definition of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, any other descendant of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> of degree at least <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> must be a neighbour of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, by minimality of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, we can suppose that all its other neighbours of degree <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> cannot fall into the previous cases. Thus, we can suppose that all its other neighbour have degree at most <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, and if they have degree <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, they are adjacent to a path of length two and a path of length at most <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, we recall that by minimality of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, there cannot be a path of length at least <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> attached to <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<ul id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:11pt;">
<li id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">*</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume first <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G}(v)\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G}(v)\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}=G-T_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}=G-T_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> an optimal edge-irregulator of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> can be a path, but a path has an edge-irregulator of size at most <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{|G^{\prime}|}{3}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{|G^{\prime}|}{3}+1</annotation></semantics></math> which will be sufficient here. Let <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=S^{\prime}\cup\{vv^{\prime}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=S^{\prime}\cup\{vv^{\prime}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is easy to check that <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-S</annotation></semantics></math> is indeed locally irregular. Since exactly one edge adjacent to <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> was removed, its resulting degree is at least <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, all its neighbours have degree at most <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> and the rest of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> was already locally irregular. Moreover, <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> contains at least <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math> edges. Thus <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S|=|S^{\prime}|+1\leq\frac{|G^{\prime}|}{3}+2\leq\frac{|G|-7}{3}+2\leq\frac{|G|}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S|=|S^{\prime}|+1\leq\frac{|G^{\prime}|}{3}+2\leq\frac{|G|-7}{3}+2\leq\frac{|G|}{3}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">*</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(v)=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(v)=3</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> be the neighbour of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> other than <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. We consider <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}=G-T_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}=G-T_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a path, then <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{g}(w)=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{g}(w)=3</annotation></semantics></math>, as otherwise <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> would be a bistar. In this case, consider <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> an edge-irregulator of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of size at most <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{|G^{\prime}|}{3}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{|G^{\prime}|}{3}+1</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=S^{\prime}\cup\{vv^{\prime}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=S^{\prime}\cup\{vv^{\prime}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. As in the previous case, since there are at least <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> edges in <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and since <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is already locally irregular, we are done. Otherwise, <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a path and we can consider <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> an optimal edge-irregulator of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of size at most <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{|G^{\prime}|}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{|G^{\prime}|}{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G}(w)\in\{1,3\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G}(w)\in\{1,3\}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=S^{\prime}\cup\{vv^{\prime}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=S^{\prime}\cup\{vv^{\prime}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge-irregulator of size at most <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{|G|}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{|G|}{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. Otherwise, <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G}(w)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G}(w)=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote by <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> the child of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a leaf, since we have already proved that there is no path of length at least <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> adjacent to <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\cup\{vv^{\prime},ww^{\prime}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\cup\{vv^{\prime},ww^{\prime}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge-irregulator of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> of size at most <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{|G|}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{|G|}{3}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|T_{v}|\geq 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|T_{v}|\geq 6</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">*</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We can finally assume <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(v)=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(v)=4</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> be the two other children of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. W.l.o.g., suppose <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(w)\geq d(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(w)\geq d(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. By hypothesis on <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3=d(u)\geq d(w)\geq d(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3=d(u)\geq d(w)\geq d(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, and both <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> can only have paths as descendents. In case of equality in their degrees, suppose <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|T_{u}|\geq|T_{w}|\geq|T_{x}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|T_{u}|\geq|T_{w}|\geq|T_{x}|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<ul id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:10pt;">
<li id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">·</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(w)=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(w)=3</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}=G\setminus T_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}=G\setminus T_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a path, <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is already locally irregular. Indeed, all the vertices of degree <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> or more are <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> and some of its neighbours. Thus, using Claim <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem6" title="Claim 4.6 ‣ Proof ‣ 4.1 Trees ‣ 4 Structural results ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>, and since all the neighbours or <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> have degree at most <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, there are no conflicts in <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
Otherwise, let <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be an edge-irregulator of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> be an optimal edge-irregulator of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a path and <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a minimum counter-example, we have <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S^{\prime}|\leq\frac{|G^{\prime}|}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S^{\prime}|\leq\frac{|G^{\prime}|}{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. Also, <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S_{x}|\leq\left\lceil\frac{|T_{x}|}{3}\right\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S_{x}|\leq\left\lceil\frac{|T_{x}|}{3}\right\rceil</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is a path. Consider <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=S^{\prime}\cup\{vv^{\prime},vx\}\cup S_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=S^{\prime}\cup\{vv^{\prime},vx\}\cup S_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. By construction, <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge-irregulator of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S|\leq\frac{|G|}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S|\leq\frac{|G|}{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, see Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1 Trees ‣ 4 Structural results ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(a).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">·</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G}(w)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G}(w)=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G}(x)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G}(x)=2</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}=G\setminus(T_{u}\cup T_{w}\cup T_{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}=G\setminus(T_{u}\cup T_{w}\cup T_{x})</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be an optimal edge-irregulator of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G^{\prime}-S^{\prime}}(v^{\prime})\neq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G^{\prime}-S^{\prime}}(v^{\prime})\neq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G^{\prime}-S^{\prime}}(v)=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G^{\prime}-S^{\prime}}(v)=4</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that
<math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is also an edge-irregulator of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and has size at most <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{G}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>G</mi><mn>3</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{G}{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. Otherwise, let <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> be an optimal edge-irregulator of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> is either a path on <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> or on <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> vertices, which implies <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S_{u}|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S_{u}|=1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S_{u}|=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S_{u}|=2</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Consider <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=S^{\prime}\cup S_{u}\cup\{(vu)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=S^{\prime}\cup S_{u}\cup\{(vu)\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge-regulator of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S|=|S^{\prime}|+1+|S_{u}|\leq\frac{|G^{\prime}|}{3}+1+|S_{u}|\leq\frac{|G|}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0em">+</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false" lspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S|=|S^{\prime}|+1+|S_{u}|\leq\frac{|G^{\prime}|}{3}+1+|S_{u}|\leq\frac{|G|}{3}</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|T_{v}|=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|T_{v}|=8</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|T_{u}|=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|T_{u}|=4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|T_{v}|=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|T_{v}|=9</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|T_{u}|=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|T_{u}|=5</annotation></semantics></math>, see Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1 Trees ‣ 4 Structural results ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(b).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">·</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, suppose <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G}(w)\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G}(w)\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G}(x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G}(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}=G\setminus T_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}=G\setminus T_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>. Once again, if <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a path, <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is already locally irregular. Let <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be an edge-irregulator of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=S^{\prime}\cup\{vv^{\prime},vx\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=S^{\prime}\cup\{vv^{\prime},vx\}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(w)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(w)=2</annotation></semantics></math>, we also add to <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> the edge connecting <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> to its leaf. In both cases, <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge-irregulator of <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> of size at most <math id="S4.I5.i2.I1.i2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{G}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>G</mi><mn>3</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{G}{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, see Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1 Trees ‣ 4 Structural results ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(c).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="Thmproofx11.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, a minimal counterexample does not have a vertex of degree at least <math id="Thmproofx11.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning it is a path, which concludes the proof.</p>
</div>
