<div id="S4.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.7</span></span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a tree of order <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and maximum degree <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, then computing <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> can be done in time <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n\Delta^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n\Delta^{3})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproofx12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmproofx12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, we will compute recursively, for each vertex <math id="Thmproofx12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, two arrays of size at most <math id="Thmproofx12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> containing the minimum number of edges in an edge-irregulator of the subtree induced by <math id="Thmproofx12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, with or without the edge to its parent, for any possible degree for <math id="Thmproofx12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>. We fix a root for <math id="Thmproofx12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, and denote <math id="Thmproofx12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> the subtree induced by <math id="Thmproofx12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmproofx12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> its parent. We denote <math id="Thmproofx12.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{u}</annotation></semantics></math> the array that will contain, at the <math id="Thmproofx12.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th position, the minimum number of edges in an edge-irregulator <math id="Thmproofx12.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmproofx12.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> under the assumption that <math id="Thmproofx12.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="Thmproofx12.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmproofx12.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{u}\cup(pu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{u}\cup(pu)</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, we denote <math id="Thmproofx12.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{u}</annotation></semantics></math> the array that will contain, at the <math id="Thmproofx12.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th position, the minimum number of edges in an edge-irregulator <math id="Thmproofx12.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmproofx12.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> under the assumption that <math id="Thmproofx12.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="Thmproofx12.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmproofx12.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that if <math id="Thmproofx12.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is a leaf, <math id="Thmproofx12.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{u}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx12.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{u}</annotation></semantics></math> are both the array of size one containing a zero.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx12.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx12.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Now, we suppose <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is not a leaf and denote <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1},\dots,v_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1},\dots,v_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> its children. We first compute the values of <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq\delta\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq\delta\leq k</annotation></semantics></math> which will be the prescribed degree of <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> (and the value of <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{u}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{u}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>), for any <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq k</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="in(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">in(i)</annotation></semantics></math> the smallest value of <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{v_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{v_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> other than <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{v_{i}}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{v_{i}}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, we denote <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="out(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">out(i)</annotation></semantics></math> the smallest value of <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{v_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{v_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We claim that <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{u}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{u}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-\delta+\Sigma_{i\in S_{1}}in(i)+\Sigma_{i\in S_{2}}out(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-\delta+\Sigma_{i\in S_{1}}in(i)+\Sigma_{i\in S_{2}}out(i)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the subset of <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\dots,k\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dots,k\}</annotation></semantics></math> containing the values of the <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> indices <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="in(i)-out(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">in(i)-out(i)</annotation></semantics></math> is minimal, and <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the other indices. We compute each value of <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Up^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>u</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Up^{u}</annotation></semantics></math> in the exact same fashion (if <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is not the root), except that <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> will contain <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta-1</annotation></semantics></math> indexes instead of <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that if we denote <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> the root of <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is the smallest value of <math id="Thmproofx12.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{r}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx12.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmproofx12.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproofx12.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Running time.</span> We now prove that this algorithm runs in time <math id="Thmproofx12.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n\Delta^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n\Delta^{3})</annotation></semantics></math>. For each vertex, and for each degree prescription <math id="Thmproofx12.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, computing the <math id="Thmproofx12.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="in" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">in</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx12.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="out" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">out</annotation></semantics></math> functions, as well as the values <math id="Thmproofx12.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="in-out" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">in-out</annotation></semantics></math>, can be done in time <math id="Thmproofx12.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\Delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\Delta)</annotation></semantics></math>. Searching the <math id="Thmproofx12.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> smallest values of <math id="Thmproofx12.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="in-out" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">in-out</annotation></semantics></math> can be done in time <math id="Thmproofx12.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\Delta^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\Delta^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, hence the final complexity.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx12.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmproofx12.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproofx12.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correctness.</span> Note that reaching degree <math id="Thmproofx12.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmproofx12.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> implies choosing <math id="Thmproofx12.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> children such that the edge between <math id="Thmproofx12.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and said child in not deleted. In particular, if we denote by <math id="Thmproofx12.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> the set of children adjacent to <math id="Thmproofx12.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx12.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> the set of children not adjacent to <math id="Thmproofx12.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, then the size of an optimal edge-irregulator of <math id="Thmproofx12.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> is smaller than <math id="Thmproofx12.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i\in S_{1}}in(i)+\Sigma_{i\in S_{2}}out(i)+d_{G}(u)-\delta=d_{G}(u)-\delta+\Sigma_{i\in S_{1}}in(i)+\Sigma_{i\in S_{2}}out(i)=d_{G}(u)-\delta+\Sigma_{i}in(i)-\Sigma_{i\in S_{2}}in(i)+\Sigma_{i}out(i)-\Sigma_{i\in S_{1}}out(i)=d_{G}(u)-\delta+\Sigma_{i\in S_{1}}(in(i)-out(i))+\Sigma_{i}out(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i\in S_{1}}in(i)+\Sigma_{i\in S_{2}}out(i)+d_{G}(u)-\delta=d_{G}(u)-\delta+\Sigma_{i\in S_{1}}in(i)+\Sigma_{i\in S_{2}}out(i)=d_{G}(u)-\delta+\Sigma_{i}in(i)-\Sigma_{i\in S_{2}}in(i)+\Sigma_{i}out(i)-\Sigma_{i\in S_{1}}out(i)=d_{G}(u)-\delta+\Sigma_{i\in S_{1}}(in(i)-out(i))+\Sigma_{i}out(i)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx12.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx12.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Since the values <math id="Thmproofx12.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}out(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}out(i)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmproofx12.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G}(u)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx12.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> are fixed, choosing the minimal value for <math id="Thmproofx12.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i\in S_{1}}(in(i)-out(i))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i\in S_{1}}(in(i)-out(i))</annotation></semantics></math> yields an optimal edge-irregulator of <math id="Thmproofx12.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Complete graphs</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote by <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> the complete graph of order <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Complete graphs are an interested case for our problem, as computing <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}(K_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}(K_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is the same as finding the size of locally irregular graphs of order <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> with the most edges.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In what follows, we are solving the question for particular values of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, while we only provide partial results for the remaining ones. Precisely, we focus on the case where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">triangular number</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, of the form <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{k(k+1)}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{k(k+1)}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote by <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> the <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> triangular number, being the sum of all integers from <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> up to <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=t_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=t_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a triangular number,
then the maximum number of edges in a locally irregular graph of order <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is exactly <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{k}:=\frac{k(k+1)(k-1)(3k+2)}{24}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>24</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{k}:=\frac{k(k+1)(k-1)(3k+2)}{24}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproofx13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmproofx13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmproofx13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a locally irregular graph of order <math id="Thmproofx13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=t_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=t_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
First, note that, for every <math id="Thmproofx13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,\dots,k\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,\dots,k\}</annotation></semantics></math>, there are at most <math id="Thmproofx13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> vertices of degree <math id="Thmproofx13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-k</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmproofx13.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, otherwise, by the pigeonhole principle, two of them would be adjacent. So, any locally irregular graph of order <math id="Thmproofx13.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> having the most edges has one vertex of degree <math id="Thmproofx13.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math>, two vertices of degree <math id="Thmproofx13.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-2</annotation></semantics></math>, and so on.
Because <math id="Thmproofx13.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is a triangular number, such a graph <math id="Thmproofx13.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> can be constructed for all <math id="Thmproofx13.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>; we prove this by induction on <math id="Thmproofx13.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx13.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx13.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The graph <math id="Thmproofx13.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the graph containing a single vertex. Then, for any <math id="Thmproofx13.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmproofx13.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has been constructed, we obtain <math id="Thmproofx13.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="Thmproofx13.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> by adding <math id="Thmproofx13.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i+1</annotation></semantics></math> independent vertices adjacent to all the vertices of <math id="Thmproofx13.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is easy to see that for any <math id="Thmproofx13.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, the graph <math id="Thmproofx13.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> indeed has the claimed degree sequence. To fully prove the claim, it remains to express the number of edges of any <math id="Thmproofx13.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. The starting point is the handshaking lemma, which states that in any graph the number of edges is twice the sum of the degrees:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2|E(T_{k})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2|E(T_{k})|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\underset{i=1}{\overset{k}{\sum}}i(n-i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑘</mo></munderover><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\underset{i=1}{\overset{k}{\sum}}i(n-i)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=n\underset{i=1}{\overset{k}{\sum}}i-\underset{i=1}{\overset{k}{\sum}}i^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑘</mo></munderover><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑘</mo></munderover><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=n\underset{i=1}{\overset{k}{\sum}}i-\underset{i=1}{\overset{k}{\sum}}i^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left(\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\right)^{2}-\frac{k(k+1)(2k+1)}{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>6</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left(\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\right)^{2}-\frac{k(k+1)(2k+1)}{6}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right"><span id="S4.Ex6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">since <math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=\frac{k(k+1)}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=\frac{k(k+1)}{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\left(\frac{k(k+1)}{2}-\frac{2k+1}{3}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\left(\frac{k(k+1)}{2}-\frac{2k+1}{3}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\frac{3k^{2}-k-2}{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>6</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\frac{3k^{2}-k-2}{6}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{k(k+1)(k-1)(3k+2)}{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>12</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{k(k+1)(k-1)(3k+2)}{12}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproofx13.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This concludes the proof.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.8</span></span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=t_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=t_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}(K_{n})=|E(K_{n})|-m_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}(K_{n})=|E(K_{n})|-m_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Even though Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 4.8 ‣ 4.2 Complete graphs ‣ 4 Structural results ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a> is tight, it cannot be generalised easily to complete graphs whose order is not a triangular number. However, since <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{k+1}-t_{k}=k=\Theta(\sqrt{t_{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{k+1}-t_{k}=k=\Theta(\sqrt{t_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma4" title="Lemma 4 ‣ 4.2 Complete graphs ‣ 4 Structural results ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> yields both a lower and an upper bound on the size of locally irregular graphs of order <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> having the most edges, when <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is not triangular: namely, they have <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{n^{2}}{2}-O(n^{3/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{n^{2}}{2}-O(n^{3/2})</annotation></semantics></math> edges. And from this, we proved that Conjecture <a href="#Thmconjecture1" title="Conjecture 1 ‣ 2 Preliminaries, first results and a conjecture ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> holds for all complete graphs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we extended the study of edge-irregulators from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. Apart from focusing on the algorithmic aspects of this problem, we also derived general bounds and analysed behaviours of optimal edge-irregulators, leading to Conjecture <a href="#Thmconjecture1" title="Conjecture 1 ‣ 2 Preliminaries, first results and a conjecture ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, which we verified for several graph classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Determining <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> for a graph <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> remains challenging due to the intricate properties and elusive structure of maximally edge-dense locally irregular graphs. Let us denote by <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> (for some <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>) the class of all locally irregular graphs of order <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> with the most edges possible. In light of Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma4" title="Lemma 4 ‣ 4.2 Complete graphs ‣ 4 Structural results ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, a key open question concerns the size and structural properties of <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> in the case where <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-triangular number.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We finally observe that there is room for improvement for many of our results. This could lead to further work on the topic. In particular, we find the following questions appealing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Refining the static parameter <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm conf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>conf</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm conf}</annotation></semantics></math> (Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Structural results ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) to dynamically capture evolving edge conflicts.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Determining <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> for complete graphs and tightening bounds for cubic graphs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Extending Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 2.1 ‣ 2 Preliminaries, first results and a conjecture ‣ Graph Irregularity via Edge Deletions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> to subcubic graphs. Also, for <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-degenerate graphs <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> of order <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, we could get an upper bound on <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> of about <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k-1)n+\left\lfloor\frac{n}{3}\right\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k-1)n+\left\lfloor\frac{n}{3}\right\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> through exploiting similar ideas to the ones used in this paper.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">It is also interesting to further study the algorithmic complexity of determining <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> for a given graph <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, the authors of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> proved that the vertex counterpart of the problem is in <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{FPT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖥𝖯𝖳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{FPT}</annotation></semantics></math> when parameterized by the treewidth and the maximum degree. Their algorithm should adapt in an obvious way to our context. Thus, we prefer to not include such a result here.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We finish by commenting on the <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{FPT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖥𝖯𝖳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{FPT}</annotation></semantics></math> algorithm we present here w.r.t. the vertex cover number. First, observe that there is still room for improvement in its running time. Indeed, we are only aware of a <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{o(vc)}n^{O(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{o(vc)}n^{O(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> lower bound (based on the ETH) for this problem (stemming as a direct corollary of the <math id="S5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{W}}[1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖶</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{W}}[1]</annotation></semantics></math>-hardness w.r.t. the solution size proof presented in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>). Lastly, we believe that this algorithm could be generalised to work w.r.t. the neighbourhood diversity of the input graph. However, in order to achieve this, it would be mandatory to completely determine <math id="S5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm I}_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm I}_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> for complete graphs.</p>
</div>
</section>
